00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:18.390 align:start position:0%
 
you  hi my name is Melissa Hendershot and I'm  a graduate student at UCSF today I'm  here with Chris Garcia who's a professor  and Howard Hughes Medical Investigator  at Stanford University chris is a  dedicated professor but he also spent  thousands of hours training for and  running ultra marathons so welcome Chris  thanks for joining us thank you first of  all can you just tell us a little bit  about what an ultra marathon is and what  that entails technically an ultra  marathon is any distance race longer  than a marathon so ultra marathons can  be anything from fifty kilometers which  is a pretty popular distance 200 miles  and you know they're races in between 50  mile or hundred K's  so that's that's what defines an ultra  marathon when did you first start  running seriously well I was always an  athlete I was a tennis player growing up  a pretty serious tennis player and then  in graduate school I started running and  I started running marathons in the East  Coast and actually wrote a single scull  for a while when I was in graduate  school and then when I moved to  California for my postdoc I started  getting more serious about running and  doing triathlons and and I did a lot of  triathlons when I was in San Diego for  my postdoc at Scripps and you know I'd  always heard about ultra marathons and  I'd always had a predilection for  distance and and so and I knew that  ultra marathons were a big thing in the  Bay Area but you know like 15 years ago  when I was just hearing about them they  weren't as popular as they are now so it  was kind of an urban myth you know these  races where people would run a hundred  miles and and and so that's you know and  so that I felt like that was something  that I could potentially be decent at  and it kind of escalated from there  what was your first ultra so my first  ultra was actually a postdoc in my lab  had had run some ultras and and she got  me to sign up with her to run a race and  the Marin Headlands called the Marin 50k  and it's through the headlands and it  was through the trails and it was just  it was just a it was almost like an  out-of-body experience it was just you  know running through the fog over there  and along the coastal trail and through  the then through the the rain forests  and it was from the first time I ever  did it it was just that was it  I was hooked and that was you know  almost 12 years ago how many ultra  marathons have you done do you know yeah  I've done a total of 80 80 or 82 or  something like that and amongst those 80  some races I think I've run like 1,300  miles probably 25 or 30 50 miles and ten  or so hundred K's and then a lot of 50k  so kind of you know the very long  distance races you run fewer of this  your body just can't take that many how  do you train for their races so during  the week I don't you know I do normal  distances you know I'll run six or seven  miles maybe four to four times a week  and actually do a lot of cross-training  I swim a lot I swim with Stanford  master's team a lot in the mornings and  I also cycle a lot and so I have like  the 25 mile loop I do to get to work  just to sort of take the pounding off my  body  but then on weekends there's there's  usually a long Saturday run that's  anywhere from 20 to 30 miles with a  group of people I've been running with  for a long time they're they're great  friends and a big part of why I'm doing  this and that that's a you know  saturday-morning staple and then as  we're sort of ramping up into races and  now you'll back it up again on Sunday  with maybe like a 15 or 20  run so the key to doing ultras the long  run and the long run can be anywhere  from twenty to forty miles and then  backing it up with the long run the next  day and so it comes in large chunks you  know and so for me going into a hundred  mile er  you know I might max out it like an 80  or 90 mile week for a few weeks but  average total maybe 60 or 70 miles a  week some people run a hundred and fifty  miles a week you know the real the real  elite ultra runners you know will run a  100 150 200 miles a week some people can  get away with less and so it's a very  individual thing how do you manage to  push yourself past the feelings of  discomfort and unhappiness yeah that's a  good question you just keep going you  know you realize you know when you first  start doing these races you really doubt  what you can do and and and you fear  failure and and but then with enough  experience you realize that you can get  passed through those those bad patches  when you're when you're just completely  you just can't go on that you can you  know if you understand your nutrition  well enough you know how to manage  yourself hydrate yourself and you know  you have the confidence you've gone  those longer distances before you can  push yourself through it and you recover  it's amazing in these races how many  times you you you go to complete failure  and you recover and then you're running  along just as if you started running you  know ten minutes ago and and so it's a  constant sort of cycle of ups and downs  in these races what sorts of things do  you think about when you're running you  know it's interesting um well you know  when I'm running just for for for  training or you know in the afternoon  here at Stanford and like out here I'm  usually thinking about problems in the  lab science projects issues with people  managing projects but then when you run  far enough you your your mind goes blank  and you don't really think about  anything you just kind of you don't have  enough glucose in your brain to think  and  and you know people ask me you know what  do you think about during 100-mile race  when you're out there for a day do you  get bored and it's amazing how fast  these things go I mean I barely remember  anything that happens in these races you  know you're just kind of in survival  mode and you're thinking about managing  yourself and your nutrition and and so  you're just you're so in the moment and  you're not thinking about anything else  except just you know maximizing you know  how well how good you feel have you ever  had a scientific breakthrough moment  while you've been out running on the  trail yeah actually a lot of them I've  had a lot of ideas for projects usually  usually early in the run and I remember  very distinctly when I was an assistant  professor we we were trying to solve a  crystal structure that had a very  complex packing and symmetry problem and  you know we were banging on the thing  for a week or two and and well the  project took longer than that but but  once we had phases we know we were  staring at the graphics and we couldn't  figure out the symmetry of the thing and  and I took a run around Stanford tonight  and I and I solved the problem in my  head it just came to me you know it just  kind of percolate it up and so you know  stuff like that happens all the time how  have you found ways to balance having  both a demanding profession and a pretty  demanding hobby  well I'm very disciplined with my time  and I I don't have a lot of free time  I'm I'm usually doing one of two things  I'm either working or I'm I'm out  training and you know I I get up at 4:00  in the morning and I'm I I work for a  while and then I swim and then usually  you know then I go to work and then I go  to lab and and then later in the  afternoon I usually do another training  session and then go back to lab and then  go home and I don't get a lot of sleep  and  I enjoy it I mean I love it I love I  love the immersion you know I love my  life and and and there's not a lot of  there's not a lot of tolerant you know  the tolerances are pretty fine between  the stuff I'm doing and I always feel  like it's on the verge of blowing up but  but I feel very fortunate you know  there's so much there's so many things I  want to do that  you know there's it's difficult to find  enough time in the day often do you see  any parallels between running ultra  marathons and running a lab or any  skills that you've acquired through your  racing that have helped you as a P I  definitely it's been hugely enormous ly  helpful I mean just athletics in general  has a lot of lessons to learn about  doing science you know and and you know  there's a physicality to science that I  think people tend to underestimate that  you know you need to be able to push  yourself through barriers to keep going  on hard scientific problems and ultra  teaches a lot of lessons about humility  and stoicism staying within oneself and  and being in the moment it's had a great  pacifying influence on me for sure and  also having the long view you know  science when you if you're working on  hard scientific problems or any sort of  important scientific problem it's  generally going to be a long-term  problem and and you have to have the  patience and the foresight to understand  that the payoff is in the future it's in  the distance and it's also in the  process of doing the science it's not  just in the reward so ultra has I think  the same kinds of lessons to teach that  about about you know enjoying the  process learning learning from the  process and and having the long view and  having the confidence that you can get  there if you just persist  you

